  "user_scenario": "Rolando Jessop is seventy-one and rings on a Tuesday afternoon from his kitchen in Wexham, where he has spread out the envelopes that came with his plan papers. He is not cross, only puzzled. \"A card came with my plan papers back in September and nobody has told me what it is for. I want to know whether I am owed anything on it.\" That is the whole of what he wants, and he says it in the first half minute. He moved onto this plan in the spring after coming back to the area, and he mentions that only as the reason the papers were late. He does not ask for anything to be done to the card and would not know what to ask for; he is content for the plan to do whatever its own side of the work is, and says so if he is asked for that.\n\nHe gives his name, Rolando Jessop, and when the representative asks how to find him he reads out the telephone number on his bill, 4155504418. For anything further he offers his date of birth, 1955-02-17, and the identifier on his Medicare card, 3JL5RK9TN62, which he spells out letter by letter because, he says, the last person who took it down got it wrong. He will give the address, 77 Pickering Lane, Wexham, or the ZIP, 94121, if one of those is wanted instead.\n\nAsked whether he has the card itself, he says it is here on the table and reads the last four digits, 8802, adding that the rest of the number is embossed but he will not read it out over the telephone to anybody. He has no idea what is on the card and says so plainly if he is asked what he expects: nobody has ever sent him a statement for it, and he has never tried it at a till. Asked when the envelope came he says the first week of September, with the plan's own letter. Asked whether he has spent anything with it, he says no, not a penny. Asked about the card he had the year before, he says that one finished with the old plan and he threw it away at Christmas.\n\nIf the representative explains that bringing the card into use is something only he can do and hands it over to him, he does it there and then, reading back the figures and the reference he is given and confirming the last four digits on the card once more. He is slow but careful and will repeat anything that was not heard. If the representative tells him that something has to be put right on the card's record before anything can be put on it, he says to go ahead and do whatever is needed.\n\nIf he is asked where his statements should go he says paper and email both, the way the plan has had it since he joined. If he is asked about the medical plan premium he says it goes out by cheque every month and he has never missed one.\n\nWhen he is told what has been put on the card for the periods gone by, he says that is a good deal better than he expected, thanks the representative and says he will try it at the chemist in the morning. He asks for nothing else and raises nothing the representative has not raised with him.",
